# x86 port I/O: refused by design on the cross lanes (plugs 1.50,
# 2026-08-20). The compile must FAIL and the log must carry
# "[UNSUPPORTED] <name>" for each line below.
#
# THIS ONE IS NOT ARCHITECTURAL, and it is the only sidecar here that is
# not. The other three test x86 itself: port-in-32-width is about the
# width of an x86 `in`, vbe-mode-set about a BIOS video extension,
# dhcp-acquire about an NE2000 behind codex-vm's NAT. Their subjects
# cannot exist on ARM64 or RISC-V and refusing them costs no coverage.
#
# This test's subject is __heap-save / __heap-restore bracket PLACEMENT,
# which is a property of the allocator and is fully portable. The CMOS
# RTC on 0x70/0x71 is only its INSTRUMENT: the chapter picks it because
# it is an effectful bind answering an Integer that no device need be
# present for and that the compiler cannot fold. Nothing about the
# question being asked is x86.
#
# It is refused here because neither cross lane has ANY effectful
# non-foldable Integer source to put in the RTC's place: no clock, no
# tick, no counter, no random (arm64 and riscv runtimes, 2026-08-20).
# So the allocator-bracket question goes unasked on both lanes, and this
# sidecar records that as a gap rather than as correct-by-design.
# Give either lane such a source and this test should be made portable
# and the sidecar deleted.
port-in-byte
port-out-byte
